The thickness of the Al 4 C 3  layer that forms between a carbon fiber and aluminum is generally described and can be estimated using diffusion-controlled reaction kinetics – and depends on (infiltration) temperature, dwell time, and interfacial reaction kinetics. As the carbide layer forms, further growth is governed by the diffusion of carbon atoms through the already existing Al₄C₃ layer, which leads to a characteristic parabolic growth behavior. This behavior is typically expressed by the parabolic growth law, described in Eq. ( 1 ): 1 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
